  <DataNode TextLabel="IDL" GraphId="f5e41" Type="Pathway" GroupRef="b8804">
    <Comment>A triglyceride-rich lipoprotein particle that typically contains APOB100, APOE and APOCs and has a density of 1.006-1.019 g/ml and a diameter of between 25-30 nm. IDL particles are found in blood and are formed by the delipidation of very-low-density lipoprotein particles (VLDL). IDL particles are removed from blood by the liver, following binding to the APOE receptor, or are converted to low-density lipoprotein (LDL).</Comment>
    <Graphics CenterX="3098.3949556750304" CenterY="302.6400091895452" Width="110.21594127506263" Height="35.0" ZOrder="28917" FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="15" Valign="Middle" ShapeType="RoundedRectangle" Color="9999ff"/>
    <Xref Database="GeneOntology" ID="GO:0034363"/>
  </DataNode>
